WEBVTT

00:00.720 --> 00:05.960
Currently, we waste about one-third of everything that’s produced,
and that’s globally.

00:09.280 --> 00:12.000
Now, I believe this is going to become
one of the biggest issues.

00:12.080 --> 00:17.160
Right now, when they go to the climate summits,
they aren’t talking about food waste.

00:17.220 --> 00:21.200
But this is where we could really make a big difference.
We waste one-third of everything we produce.

00:23.200 --> 00:26.860
In rich countries, it’s because food is so cheap,
we can throw it away. Right?

00:28.640 --> 00:33.560
Servings are great big. Go to one of these restaurants,
the platter comes out. It’s an amazing amount of food.

00:33.720 --> 00:35.820
You might not eat all of it.
It gets discarded.

00:36.420 --> 00:43.240
In poorer countries, it’s because there’s no infrastructure
to get the crops out of the field. There’s no refrigeration, so the stuff spoils.

00:43.880 --> 00:51.040
So, we produce one-third more than we really need
right now with food waste.

00:51.340 --> 00:56.280
In other words, we grow enough food to feed everybody.
It’s a matter of economics, infrastructure, and distribution.

00:56.920 --> 01:03.420
This is something that’s going to be, I think, a bigger issue moving forward,
and it wouldn’t hurt agriculture to hop on this issue.

01:03.660 --> 01:09.920
When we’re being attacked about the amount of water we use
in California’s dairies, or the amount of methane that we produce,

01:10.060 --> 01:16.300
or the amount of diesel that we burn, maybe we should go right to food waste
and say, “Yes, but consumer, this is on you.”

01:16.620 --> 01:19.840
All we’re doing is producing the food.
It’s not us that’s wasting it.
